{"text": "Resume\nJeanette Kowalik, PhD, MPH, MCHES\nWashington, DC 20008\ntt\n|\n\nSummary of Qualifications\n115 years of experience in public health, non-profit organizations, and academia:\n\u00a9 7 years senior-level management in non-profit settings\n+10 years personnel management (e.g. direct service, clinicians, managers, interns, and\nfellows)\n\u201812 years of adjunct teaching experience; 3 at the graduate-level and 2 remote\n+10 years budget management ranging from $800,000 to three million/ year\n+ Exceptional experience in grants and contracts (e.g. led grant writing efforts to earn\n$26 million to date)\n\u2018+ Maintained productive relationships with various entities including the Department of\nHealth and Human Services (e.g. HRSA, CDC, ACF) and foundations (e.g. Robert Wood\nJohnson Foundation, W.K. Kellogg Foundation)\n* Strong workforce development and quality improvement background associated with\nthe Public Health Accreditation Board, local public health department accreditation\n(e.g. Chicago Department of Public Health)\n* Firm believer in mentorship, sponsorship, and coaching: giving and receiving\nLed national-level population health initiatives such as the Association of American\nUniversities Sexual Assault Climate survey at the University of Wisconsin (UW)-Madison\n* Active national public health leader through the American Public Health Association-\nHealth Administration and Maternal and Child Health Sections\n* Trained as an advanced Public Information Officer for crisis response via FEMA\n* Appointed to two non-profit boards serving youth and families and the Washington\nD.C. Commission on Out of School Time Grants and Youth Outcomes (3 year\nappointments)\n\u00abDeveloped part-time consulting business for projects aside from role at a national\nnon-profit organization as a means to give back\n\nMy areas of expertise are leadership, equity, growth, deepening impact, program\ndevelopment and evaluation; strategic planning; technical assistance; workforce development;\ndevelopment including grants and contracts (application and administration); coalitions and\npartnerships.\n\n\u2018My passions are the arts, youth, women/ LGBTQIA/ disability rights, dismantling racism, K-22\npipeline programs (e.g. STEM), education, leadership, and public health.\n\nWork History\nDistrict of Columbia, Commission on Out of School Time Grants and Youth Outcomes,\n\nWashington D.C.",
